To make the sidebar collapse and expand automatically based on the screen size, you need to use media queries in CSS. Media queries allow you to apply different styles when the screen size changes. You can define different breakpoints for different screen sizes, such as desktop, tablet, and mobile. 

Here is an example of how you can achieve this effect:

HTML:

```html
<header>
  <div class="header-container">
    <!-- your menu items -->
    <div class="menu">
      <button class="menu-toggle">
        <i class="fa fa-bars"></i>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>
```

CSS:

```css
.header-container {
  min-width: 768px; /* this is your breakpoint for desktop and tablet view */
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

.menu {
  position: fixed; /* make sure the menu is fixed in place */
  background: #endpoint;
  border: 1px solid #212121;
  box-shadow: 0px 0px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
  padding: 10px;
  display: flex;
  flex-column;
  list-style: none;
  width: 100%;
  z-index: 2;
}

.menu-toggle {
  cursor: pointer;
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  outline: none;
  font-size: 24px;
  color: #212121;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  font-weight: bold;
  height: 60px;
}

.menu-toggle:focus {
  outline: 0;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
  .header-container {
    min-width: 200px; /* this is your breakpoint for mobile view */
  }
  .menu {
    position: fixed; /* make sure the menu is fixed in place */
    background: #endpoint;
    border: 1px solid #212121;
    box-shadow: 0px 0px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
    padding: 10px;
    display: none;
  }
  .menu li {
    display: block;
  }
  .menu-toggle {
    z-index: 2;
    right: 20px;
    bottom: 20px;
  }
}
```

This code will make the sidebar collapse when the screen width is less than or equal to 768px (the breakpoint for mobile view). The menu will be displayed as a dropdown list. 

Please replace `.menu li` with your menu items and `.menu-toggle` with your actual menu toggle button (if you have one). 

When the screen width is greater than 768px (the breakpoint for desktop and tablet view), the sidebar will be displayed normally. When the width is less than or equal to 768px, the sidebar will be hidden and the menu items will be displayed as a dropdown list. 

Remember to specify the breakpoints according to your needs. You can add or remove breakpoints and adjust the media queries to fit your desired layout.

Calibrating a DHT22 sensor typically involves comparing its readings to a reference (like another calibrated sensor or instrument) and then making adjustments to the readings to improve accuracy. Here's an example code for calibrating a DHT22 sensor using an Arduino. You'll need the "DHT.h" library installed, which you can get from the Arduino Library Manager in the Arduino IDE.

```cpp
#include "DHT.h"

#define DHTPIN 2         // Define the pin connected to the DHT22 sensor
#define DHTTYPE DHT22    // Define the sensor type (DHT22)

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

// Calibration factors (adjust these values based on your reference sensor or instrument)
float temperatureCalibrationFactor = 1.0;
float humidityCalibrationFactor = 1.0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Start the serial communication at 9600 baud rate
  dht.begin();        // Initialize the DHT22 sensor
}

void loop() {
  delay(2000); // Wait for 2 seconds to ensure stable sensor readings

  float calTemperature = dht.readTemperature() * temperatureCalibrationFactor;
  float calHumidity = dht.readHumidity() * humidityCalibrationFactor;

  if (isnan(calTemperature) || isnan(calHumidity)) { // Check if any value is NaN
    Serial.println("Failed to read from DHT22 sensor!");
    return;
  }

  Serial.print("Calibrated Temperature: ");
  Serial.print(calTemperature);
  Serial.print("°C ");
  Serial.print("  ");
  Serial.print("Calibrated Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(calHumidity);
  Serial.println("%");
}
```

Replace `temperatureCalibrationFactor` and `humidityCalibrationFactor` variables with the calibration factors based on your reference. These factors can be calculated by comparing the DHT22 sensor's readings to a known reference sensor or instrument.

Upload the code to your Arduino and open the Serial Monitor to observe the calibrated temperature and humidity readings.

