A smart contract is a computer protocol written in code and stored on the blockchain. Smart contracts are self-executing contracts with the terms of the agreement directly written into code. They automatically execute predefined actions when certain conditions are met, which helps to facilitate, verify, or enforce a contract without the need for intermediaries.

Here is an example of a simple smart contract in Solidity, which is the primary language for writing smart contracts on Ethereum:
```solidity
// Ownable contract to represent a vending machine
contract VendingMachine {
    // Define an address to hold the funds
    address public owner;

    // Declare the initial state of the machine
    state public stock;

    // Define an event to notify of a new order
    event VendedOrder(uint256 amount, uint256 price);

    // Function to add an item to the stock
    function addItem(uint256 itemAmount, uint256 price) public {
        // Check if the item is already in the stock
        require(!stock[itemAmount]);

        // Update the stock
        stock[itemAmount] = price;

        // Transfer the ownership to the new owner
        owner = msg.sender;
    }

    // Function to sell an item for a specific amount
    function sellItem(uint256 itemAmount, uint256 amount) public {
        // Ensure the item exists in the stock
        require(stock[itemAmount]);

        // Calculate the price and update the stock
        uint256 price = stock[itemAmount] * (100 / 200);
        stock[itemAmount] = stock[itemAmount] * 200 / 100;

        // Transfer the payment to the owner
        if (msg.value > 0) {
            payable(owner).transfer(msg.value);
        }

        // Notify the owner
        emit VendedOrder(amount, price);
    }
}
```
In this example, we have created a smart contract for a vending machine. The contract has an owner (address), a stock (state), and two functions: `addItem` and `sellItem`. The `addItem` function allows users to add items to the stock, while the `sellItem` function allows users to buy items from the stock. The smart contract also emits an event `VendedOrder` to notify the owner of a new order.

This is a simple example to illustrate how a smart contract looks like. In reality, smart contracts can be more complex with additional functions, variables, and events, depending on the use case.

