In Angular, loading an NgModule into its own bundle is achieved through the use of Angular Dynamic Components (ADC). Dynamic Components allow you to lazy-load NgModules on-demand, creating separate bundles for each module. Here's how to load an NgModule into its own bundle:

1. Create an NgModule: First, ensure that you have an NgModule defined in your application. For example:
```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [AppComponent],
  imports: [BrowserModule],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule {}
```
2. Create a Dynamic Component: Next, create a dynamic component that will load the target NgModule. You will need to use the `<ng-container>` element and the `createDynamicComponent()` function from `@angular/core`. For example:
```typescript
import { Component, DynamicComponentLoader, Injector } from '@angular/core';
import { AppModule } from './app.module';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dynamic-component',
  template: `<ng-container #castle></ng-container>`
})
export class DynamicComponent implements OnInit {
  @ViewChild('castle', { read: DynamicComponentLoader }) castle: DynamicComponentLoader;

  constructor(private inj: Injector) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.castle.loadNextTo('app-dynamic-component', () => this.inj.get(AppModule)).then((fixture) => {
      // You can access the AppModule's components and services through the fixture
    });
  }
}
```
In this example, the `app-dynamic-component` component is used as the host for the loaded module. You can load the target module by specifying the selector and the `AppModule` type: `loadNextTo('app-dynamic-component', AppModule)`.
3. Use the dynamic component: In your application, use the `app-dynamic-component` component when you want to load the `AppModule`. The `AppModule` will be loaded into its own bundle, and its components and services will be available in the provided `fixture`:
```html
<div>
  <!-- Other application components -->
  <app-dynamic-component></app-dynamic-component>
</div>
```

Now, the `AppModule` is loaded into its own bundle when the `app-dynamic-component` is created. This approach allows you to lazy-load NgModules as needed and keeps the bundles separate, resulting in a more efficient application.

