In natural language processing, descriptive statements are those that simply describe a state of affairs. Analytical statements, on the other hand, are those that make an evaluation or judgment about a state of affairs.

One way to programmatically differentiate between descriptive and analytical statements is to use a rule-based approach. In this approach, you would create a set of rules that define what constitutes a descriptive statement and what constitutes an analytical statement. Then, you would apply these rules to each sentence in your corpus to determine its category.

Another way to differentiate between descriptive and analytical statements is to use a machine learning approach. In this approach, you would train a model on a corpus of labeled data. The model would then learn to classify new sentences as descriptive or analytical.

If you are interested in using a rule-based approach, the following code snippet shows how you could implement it in C#:

```c#
public class DescriptiveStatementAnalyzer
{
    public static bool IsDescriptiveStatement(string statement)
    {
        // Check if the statement is in the form of a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase.
        if (statement.Split(' ')
            .Cast<string>()
            .First()
            .IsNoun()
            && statement.Split(' ')
                .Cast<string>()
                .Skip(1)
                .First()
                .IsVerb())
        {
            return true;
        }

        // Otherwise, the statement is not a descriptive statement.
        return false;
    }
}
```

If you are interested in using a machine learning approach, the following code snippet shows how you could implement it in C#:

```c#
public class AnalyticalStatementClassifier
{
    private readonly List<string> _trainingData = new List<string>()
    {
        "80% of survey participants utilize public transportation",
        "The new iPhone is the best phone on the market",
        "I love chocolate ice cream",
        "The sky is blue",
        "2 + 2 = 4"
    };

    private readonly Dictionary<string, List<string>> _classLabels = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>()
    {
        {"descriptive", new List<string>() { "80% of survey participants utilize public transportation", "The sky is blue" }},
        {"analytical", new List<string>() { "The new iPhone is the best phone on the market", "I love chocolate ice cream", "2 + 2 = 4" }}
    };

    public bool ClassifyStatement(string statement)
    {
        // Check if the statement has already been classified.
        if (_classLabels.ContainsKey(statement))
        {
            return _classLabels[statement].Contains(statement);
        }

        // Otherwise, classify the statement using a machine learning model.
        var model = new NaiveBayesClassifier();
        model.Train(_trainingData);
        var prediction = model.Predict(statement);

        // Add the statement to the class labels dictionary.
        _classLabels[statement] = prediction ? _classLabels["analytical"] : _classLabels["descriptive"];

        return prediction;
    }
}
